/*
|--------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Guide Sections
|--------------------------------------------------------------------------
|
| The hand-written sections the long guides are made of. Real help
| centers repeat themselves: the same explanation appears in the
| getting-started guide, the warehouse guide and the troubleshooting
| guide, sometimes word for word and sometimes in an older version
| that names a screen that has since moved. Sections marked legacy
| describe the product as it was before a change; the release notes say
| when it changed. Some sections point at answers they never give.
|
*/

package stowline

/*
guideSections returns every section by key: its heading and its text.
*/
func guideSections() map[string][2]string {
	return map[string][2]string{
		"why-weight": {"Why accurate weights matter", `Every rate is priced on billable weight: the greater of what the parcel weighs and what its size says it should weigh. When the weight on the label is lower than what the carrier measures, the carrier bills the difference later as an adjustment, usually a week or two after delivery, and the adjustment is often larger than the saving because carriers round up and add a correction fee.

Weigh every parcel after packing it, with the packing material inside. A USB scale connected to the print helper fills the weight on the order automatically. If you ship the same product in the same box every time, save the packed weight on the product so imports arrive complete, and check it again whenever packaging changes. Most adjustment disputes we see come from a supplier changing a box or a filler without anyone updating the saved weight.`},
		"dim-weight": {"How dimensional weight works", `Carriers charge by space as well as weight. Dimensional weight is length × width × height divided by a divisor, 139 for most US services measured in inches and pounds, 166 for some USPS services, and 5000 for most international and European carriers measured in centimeters and kilograms. The carrier bills whichever is larger, the actual weight or the dimensional weight, after rounding each dimension up to the next whole unit.

A light product in a big box is the classic surprise: a pillow weighing 2 lb in an 18 × 14 × 10 box is billed at 19 lb on UPS Ground. Smaller boxes, poly mailers for soft goods and accurate saved package sizes are the fixes. Stowline uses the package on the order to price dimensional weight, so a wrong saved size means a wrong rate.`},
		"dim-weight-legacy": {"Dimensional weight (before 2025)", `Until January 2025 USPS only applied dimensional weight to parcels over one cubic foot, and the divisor for retail services was 194. If you are reading older invoices, adjustments before that date were calculated that way. Stowline's saved packages from that time may still have a note saying dimensional weight does not apply to small boxes; that note is out of date.`},
		"saved-packages": {"Saving your packages", `Under Settings, Packages, add every box, mailer and envelope you ship with its inside dimensions and its empty weight. Give each a name your team recognizes, such as "Small mailer" or "Shoe box", because that name appears in automation rules, on orders and in the batch screen.

Inside dimensions matter because carriers measure the outside, so add the wall thickness for corrugated boxes, about a quarter inch each side. Empty weight is added to the product weight when an order's weight comes from its items. You can import packages from a CSV with the columns name, length, width, height, unit and weight. Packages used by a rule cannot be deleted until the rule is changed.`},
		"rules-basics": {"How automation rules work", `Automation rules set things on new orders the moment they arrive: the carrier and service, the package, the warehouse, insurance, signature confirmation and tags. A rule has conditions and actions. When an order matches every condition, the actions apply.

Rules run from top to bottom, and every matching rule applies, not just the first. When two rules set the same thing, the lower rule wins. This is the opposite of how some other shipping tools work, so teams moving from them often put their most important rule at the top and are surprised when a broader rule underneath overrides it. Drag the rule you want to win to the bottom.

Rules only apply to orders imported after you save them. To apply them to orders already waiting, select those orders and choose Reapply rules.`},
		"rules-legacy-presets": {"Presets (legacy)", `Before automation rules, Stowline used presets: a saved combination of package, service and options you picked by hand when buying a label, under Settings, Shipping, Presets. Presets still work for accounts that created them, but they no longer appear for new accounts and they never apply automatically. If your team still uses presets, convert them from the automation page, which creates one rule per preset with no conditions so you can add the conditions you want.`},
		"rules-cheapest": {"Letting a rule pick the cheapest service", `Instead of naming a service, a rule's action can choose the cheapest service that meets a delivery promise, such as arriving within three business days. Stowline fetches rates from every connected carrier when the order arrives and picks the winner.

Two things surprise people. First, the label is bought at the price on the day you buy it, which can differ by a few cents from the rate when the order arrived; rates older than 24 hours are fetched again. Second, delivery promises use the carrier's own estimate, which assumes handover before the cut-off. A parcel bought at 6 p.m. and handed over the next day arrives a day later than the estimate.`},
		"rules-testing": {"Testing a rule before saving", `On the automation page, open a rule and choose Test. Paste an order number and Stowline shows which conditions matched, which did not and what the rule would change, without changing anything. Test rules against an order from each of your sales channels, because stores send fields differently: Shopify sends the shipping method title, WooCommerce sends the method id, and marketplaces often send nothing at all.`},
		"connect-carrier": {"Connecting a carrier account", `Go to Settings, Carriers, Add carrier, choose the carrier and enter the account details it asks for. Most carriers check the details straight away and Stowline fetches a test rate; when that works, the carrier's services appear in Get rates within a minute.

Some carriers check new connections before the first label: FedEx can take a business day, during which labels fail with an account pending message. Canada Post needs the contract number as well as the customer number for contract rates. Purolator issues separate test and production keys, and a test key connects without error but never buys a real label.

If you ship from several warehouses with different negotiated rates, connect one account per warehouse and limit each connection to its warehouse.`},
		"connect-carrier-legacy": {"Connecting a carrier (old screens)", `In the older dashboard, carriers were connected under Settings, Shipping, Carrier accounts, and each connection had an Advanced tab where you entered the meter number for FedEx. Meter numbers are no longer used; if your instructions mention them, follow the current steps under Settings, Carriers instead.`},
		"usps-builtin":           {"Shipping with USPS without an account", `Every Stowline account can buy USPS labels at Stowline's commercial rates without a USPS account. These labels are paid from your Stowline balance. If you have your own USPS contract with special pricing, connect it as well; Stowline's rates stay available beside it and Get rates shows which account each price comes from.`},
		"balance-basics": {"How the Stowline balance works", `Labels billed through Stowline, including every USPS label on Stowline's rates, are paid from your Stowline balance, which some older pages call the postage wallet. When the balance falls below a threshold, $25 by default, your card is charged to bring it back to the refill amount, $100 by default.

Each top-up is a separate card charge with its own receipt under Billing, Balance history. Busy stores often raise the refill amount to $500 or more so they are charged a few times a week rather than several times a day, which also stops banks flagging the charges as unusual.`},
		"balance-refunds": {"Getting money back from your balance", `Refunds for voided labels go back to your balance, not your card, because most voided labels are replaced straight away. Balance from card top-ups made in the last 120 days can be refunded to the card it came from on request; write to support with the amount. When you close your account, any remaining balance is refunded to your card automatically within 10 business days.`},
		"void-windows": {"How long you have to void a label", `Each carrier has its own window. USPS labels can be voided for 30 days and are refunded 14 to 21 days after the void, once USPS confirms the label was never scanned. UPS labels can be voided until the end-of-day close, or for 90 days if never scanned. FedEx and DHL Express labels until the first scan or pickup. Royal Mail until the end of the day of purchase. Other carriers until the first scan.

A label scanned by the carrier cannot be voided even if the parcel never left, which happens when a post office scans a pile of labels on arrival. Open a refund request from the label's page within 30 days and Stowline asks the carrier; the carrier decides, usually within 30 days.`},
		"void-how": {"Voiding labels", `Open the order, click the label and choose Void label. To void many at once, select them on the Labels page and choose Void selected. Through the API, POST to /v1/shipments/{id}/void. The order returns to Awaiting shipment so you can buy a new label straight away.`},
		"adjustments": {"Carrier adjustments", `An adjustment is an extra charge a carrier sends after handling a parcel: the measured weight or size was more than the label said, the address was residential, the carrier corrected the address, or the parcel did not qualify for the service. Adjustments for labels on your Stowline balance are taken from the balance and listed under Billing, Adjustments with the carrier's measurements next to what was on the label.

You can dispute an adjustment within 30 days. A photo of the parcel on a scale with a tape measure visible is the single most useful piece of evidence. Carriers decide most disputes within 15 business days.`},
		"adjustments-legacy": {"Adjustments (old billing page)", `Adjustments used to appear as single lines on the monthly invoice, without the order they belonged to. If you are reconciling invoices from before September 2024, export the balance history for the period; each adjustment line there has the tracking number, which you can search on the Labels page.`},
		"thermal-setup": {"Setting up a thermal printer", `Any 4x6 thermal printer works, including Zebra, Rollo, MUNBYN and DYMO 4XL. Install the printer's driver first, then add a paper size of 4 × 6 in if it is not listed. Under Settings, Printing choose 4x6 PDF to print from the browser or 4x6 ZPL with the print helper.

When printing from the browser, use 100% or Actual size, never Fit to page, and no margins. A barcode scaled down to fit can be refused at the counter.`},
		"thermal-trouble": {"When thermal labels come out wrong", `Blank labels mean the roll is upside down or the labels are not direct thermal: scratch the surface with a fingernail, and the side that darkens must face the print head. Labels cut off at the bottom mean the scale or paper size is wrong. Every other label blank means the printer has not calibrated to the gaps; hold the feed button until it feeds two labels. Faint print needs a higher darkness setting, around 20 to 25 in the driver.`},
		"print-helper":    {"The print helper", `The print helper is a small app for Windows and macOS that prints labels as soon as they are bought, with no print dialog. Download it from Settings, Printing, sign in, and choose a printer for labels and one for packing slips. It must be running on a computer that is awake; if it signs out after an update, labels queue and print when it is back. A connected USB scale also sends weights through the helper.`},
		"customs-basics": {"What a customs declaration needs", `Every parcel crossing a border needs, for each item: a description a customs officer would understand ("cotton t-shirt", not "apparel"), quantity, value in the order's currency, weight, country of origin (where it was made, not where you ship from) and a six-digit HS code. Save these on your products and they fill in automatically.

DHL Express refuses dutiable shipments without HS codes. Shipments to the EU, UK and Australia increasingly need them too, and missing codes are the most common reason parcels sit in customs.`},
		"duties": {"Who pays duties and taxes", `By default the recipient pays any duties and import taxes on delivery (DAP). Carriers often add a handling fee, and recipients who did not expect a bill may refuse the parcel, which comes back to you at your cost. With DDP you pay instead and the carrier bills you afterwards; UPS, FedEx and DHL Express support it.

For the EU, register for IOSS and add the number under Settings, Customs: the VAT you collected at checkout is then declared and the customer pays nothing more for orders under €150. For the UK, add your VAT number for orders under £135.`},
		"duties-partial": {"Duties to Switzerland and Norway", `Switzerland and Norway are outside the EU VAT system, so IOSS does not apply to them. Customers there usually pay VAT and a clearance fee on delivery. Some carriers offer a prepaid option for these countries; ask your account manager whether yours does.`},
		"returns-setup": {"Setting up returns", `The returns portal is included on Pro and Enterprise. Under Returns, Settings, turn it on, choose its address and branding, and set the rules customers see: the return window counted from delivery, products that cannot be returned (by tag or SKU), the reasons customers choose from and which ones need a photo, who pays for the label, and whether returns need approval before a label is issued.

Return labels are charged only when the carrier first scans them, so unused labels cost nothing. QR code returns let customers show a code at the counter instead of printing, for USPS, UPS and Royal Mail.`},
		"returns-resolutions": {"Refunds, exchanges and store credit", `Stowline does not move money. When a return arrives and you mark it received, Stowline can issue the refund in Shopify, BigCommerce or WooCommerce for you, or tell you to do it in your store. On Shopify and BigCommerce, customers can also choose an exchange for another size or color, or store credit with an optional bonus. Exchange orders can ship as soon as the return label is first scanned, before the return arrives.`},
		"returns-legacy":      {"Returns before the portal", `Before the returns portal, return labels were created by hand from the order with Create return label, and customers received them by email. That button still exists for one-off returns and exceptions to your rules.`},
		"tracking-gaps": {"Gaps in tracking", `Label created means the carrier has not scanned the parcel yet. That is normal until handover; a missed first scan usually shows at the next sorting center. Parcels are scanned entering and leaving facilities, not on the road, so domestic parcels can go two days without a scan and international parcels a week or more during customs.

Investigate after 5 business days without a scan for domestic ground, 2 for express and 10 for international: open the label and choose Report a problem, and Stowline opens a trace with the carrier.`},
		"delivered-not-received": {"Delivered but not received", `Ask the customer to check around the property, with neighbors, building reception and others in the household. Carriers sometimes mark a parcel delivered when it is left at a pickup point. Most turn up within two days. After that, report it from the label; carriers ask the recipient to sign a statement before investigating, and investigations take 5 to 15 business days.`},
		"claims":                 {"Filing a claim", `Stowline insurance covers loss, damage and theft up to $5,000 per parcel. File from the label page with the invoice showing what the item cost you and, for damage, photos of the item and the box. Lost parcels can be claimed after 10 days without movement (20 for international); damaged ones straight away. Claims must be filed within 60 days of the ship date and are decided within 10 business days. Claims cover your cost and the original shipping, not the retail price.`},
		"shopify-connect":        {"Connecting Shopify", `Install the Stowline app from the Shopify App Store and approve the permissions. Choose the warehouse new orders ship from and which order statuses to import. Orders appear within five minutes of being paid; Sync now imports straight away. When a label is bought, the tracking number goes back to Shopify and the order is marked fulfilled, which sends Shopify's shipping confirmation unless you turn customer notification off on the connection.`},
		"shopify-missing":        {"Shopify orders that do not appear", `Orders already fulfilled in Shopify when Stowline syncs are skipped. The usual cause is another app, or Shopify's own setting, fulfilling orders automatically at checkout. Turn automatic fulfillment off, or include fulfilled orders in the import filter. The connection's sync log lists each skipped order with the reason.`},
		"woo-connect":            {"Connecting WooCommerce", `Create a REST API key with read and write access under WooCommerce, Settings, Advanced, REST API, and paste the key and secret into Settings, Integrations, WooCommerce. Permalinks must not be set to Plain, or the REST routes do not exist. Security plugins and hosting firewalls that block the Authorization header cause a 401 even with correct keys; allow /wp-json/wc/v3 in the plugin and ask the host whether they strip the header.`},
		"woo-legacy":             {"WooCommerce plugin (retired)", `Stores connected before 2025 may still have the Stowline WooCommerce plugin installed. The plugin stopped syncing when the REST connection replaced it. Deactivate it and connect with a REST key; orders imported through the plugin stay in Stowline.`},
		"marketplaces":           {"Marketplaces: Amazon, eBay and Etsy", `Marketplaces connect by signing in from Settings, Integrations. They sync every 15 minutes. Amazon rejects tracking uploads with a ship date in the future, so buy labels on the day they ship. eBay tokens expire after 18 months; Stowline emails the owner two weeks before, and orders stop importing silently if nobody reconnects. Etsy personalization prints in full only on the long notes packing slip layout.`},
		"api-auth":               {"Authenticating API requests", `Send your key as a bearer token on every request. Live keys start with sk_live_ and buy real labels; test keys start with sk_test_ and never reach a carrier. A 401 means the key is missing or revoked; a 403 means it lacks a scope, which the message names. Keys are created under Settings, API keys and shown once.`},
		"api-idempotency":        {"Retrying safely with idempotency keys", `Network failures happen between your server and ours. If a request to buy a label times out, you cannot know whether the label was bought. Send an Idempotency-Key header derived from your own record, such as the order id, and repeat the request with the same key: if the first one succeeded, you get its result instead of a second label. Keys are kept for 24 hours. Since API version 2025-09-02, repeating a key with a different body is refused.`},
		"api-limits":             {"Rate limits", `The API allows 100 requests a second per account in live mode and 25 in test mode, 20 a second for rating and 10 for address verification. Over the limit you get 429 with Retry-After. Use batches for bulk label buying and cache checkout quotes for up to an hour per route and parcel.`},
		"webhooks-basics":        {"Receiving webhooks", `Register an HTTPS endpoint and the events you want, such as tracker.updated, refund.updated and batch.updated. Answer 2xx within 10 seconds and do the work afterwards. Deliveries can arrive out of order and more than once: use the event id to ignore repeats and occurred_at to decide which state is newest. Failed deliveries are retried with backoff for three days, after which the endpoint is disabled and the owner emailed.`},
		"webhooks-signatures":    {"Checking webhook signatures", `Each delivery has a Stowline-Signature header with a timestamp t and a signature v1: HMAC-SHA256 of t, a dot and the raw body, keyed with the endpoint's secret. Frameworks that parse JSON before you hash it change the bytes and the signature never matches; hash the raw body. Reject deliveries whose t is more than five minutes old.`},
		"team-roles":             {"Roles and permissions", `Owner, Admin, Shipper and Viewer are the built-in roles. Shippers buy, void and print labels but cannot see billing or change settings; Viewers only read. Enterprise accounts can create custom roles, such as a warehouse role that prints but never voids. Every account has one owner, the only person who can delete the account or change the owner.`},
		"sso":                    {"Single sign-on", `Enterprise accounts sign in through their identity provider with SAML 2.0. Set it up under Settings, Security, Single sign-on, test it, then require it. The owner can always sign in with a password as a break-glass account. When the identity provider rotates its signing certificate, upload the new metadata before the old certificate expires, or every sign-in fails with Signature validation failed.`},
		"two-factor":             {"Two-factor authentication", `Turn it on under Profile, Security with an authenticator app or a security key, and keep the recovery codes. Owners and admins can require it for everyone. A teammate who lost their phone can be reset by an admin; an owner who lost both phone and codes must write to support from the account's email, and verification takes up to two business days.`},
		"plans":                  {"Plans and label allowances", `Free includes 50 labels a month on USPS only. Growth, $49 a month, includes 1,500 labels, every carrier, automation rules and the API; labels over the allowance are $0.05 each. Pro, $149, includes 7,500 labels and adds the returns portal; overage is $0.04. Enterprise is priced per contract and adds single sign-on, custom roles and the audit log. Voided labels never count; return labels count only when used.`},
		"plans-legacy":           {"Plans before 2025", `Accounts that signed up before 2025 may be on the Starter plan at $29 with 500 labels, or the Business plan at $99 with 3,000. Those plans keep their price until you change plan; they cannot be chosen again once left. Their features match Growth.`},
		"failed-payment":         {"When a card is declined", `A failed plan invoice is retried on days 1, 3, 5 and 7; everything works meanwhile. After the last retry the account falls to Free plan limits until paid. A failed balance top-up is retried hourly for a day; labels billed through Stowline cannot be bought while the balance is negative, but labels on your own carrier accounts still work. Update the card under Billing, Payment method and choose Pay now or Top up now.`},
		"peak":                   {"Getting ready for peak season", `From late November carriers move cut-offs earlier, suspend delivery guarantees and add peak surcharges. Raise your balance refill amount so batches do not stall, buy large batches before 9 a.m., book daily pickups early, check printer supplies and test the print helper on every packing station. Our status page shows carrier incidents; batches retry automatically when a carrier slows down.`},
		"peak-partial":           {"Holiday shipping deadlines", `Each carrier publishes the last day to ship for delivery before December 24. We collect them on a page each November. If the page for this year is not up yet, use last year's dates minus one day as a rule of thumb.`},
		"data-export":            {"Exporting data", `Settings, Data, Export downloads orders, labels, tracking events, returns and balance history as CSV. Exports over 50,000 rows are prepared in the background and emailed. The API's reports endpoint does the same for scheduled exports. Owners can request a full export including label PDFs, delivered within a day.`},
		"delete-account":         {"Closing an account", `Only the owner can delete the account, under Settings, Account. Void unused labels first, since deleted accounts cannot void. The account is closed at once and deleted after 30 days, during which the owner can restore it. Invoices are kept for seven years as tax law requires; the remaining balance is refunded to the card.`},
		"warehouses":             {"Warehouses and ship-from addresses", `Add every place parcels leave from under Settings, Warehouses, with opening hours for pickups and an optional separate return address. Orders ship from the default warehouse unless a rule or the store's location mapping says otherwise. Warehouses with labels in the last 90 days cannot be deleted; archive them.`},
		"batches":                {"Buying labels in batches", `Select up to 1,000 orders and choose Create batch. Every order needs a weight, package and service, which rules usually set. Buying runs in the background; failed orders stay in the batch with the carrier's error so you can fix them and retry only those. Print all sorts labels by order number, SKU or service.`},
		"pickups":                {"Booking pickups", `Select labels and choose Schedule pickup, picking the warehouse and a window of at least two hours inside its opening hours. Each carrier has its own booking cut-off. USPS pickups are free with at least one Priority Mail or Ground Advantage parcel; UPS and FedEx charge an on-call fee unless you have a daily pickup. Report a missed pickup from the Pickups page and Stowline asks the carrier to come the next working day.`},
		"manifests":              {"End-of-day manifests", `A manifest closes the day's labels for one carrier account into a single barcode the driver scans, so tracking starts for every parcel at once. Manifests close automatically at 6 p.m. warehouse time; close one early from Labels, Manifests before an early pickup. Some older articles call these scan forms or end-of-day forms.`},
		"insurance-vs-declared":  {"Stowline insurance or declared value?", `UPS and FedEx include $100 of liability and sell more as declared value; claims go to the carrier under its terms, which usually exclude poorly packed items. Stowline insurance costs $0.95 per $100, covers every carrier and is claimed through Stowline. For values over $100 Stowline insurance is usually cheaper and simpler.`},
		"address-validation":     {"Address verification", `Stowline checks addresses against the carrier's database when rating. A missing unit number or a postal code from the neighboring town fails verification even when the address looks fine to a person. Accept the suggested correction, confirm with the customer, or turn on Ship to unverified address for the order if you are sure. New-build streets can take months to reach carrier databases.`},
		"address-partial":        {"Military and diplomatic addresses", `APO, FPO and DPO addresses use USPS only and need a customs declaration even though they are domestic. Some services are not available to them. Details of which services work for each military postal code are in our carrier-specific articles.`},
		"support-hours":          {"Getting help", `The messenger in the dashboard answers from the help center at any hour and passes the conversation to the team when it cannot help. The team replies within four business hours on Growth, two on Pro and one on Enterprise, from 6 a.m. to 8 p.m. Eastern on weekdays. Enterprise accounts also have a shared Slack channel and a named success manager.`},
		"status-page":            {"Carrier outages", `When a carrier's systems are down, rates and labels fail with CARRIER_UNAVAILABLE. Stowline retries batches for an hour automatically. Our status page shows incidents for Stowline and each carrier, and Enterprise accounts are notified in their shared channel.`},
		"migration-import":       {"Bringing your data from another tool", `Export saved packages, products with HS codes and your address book as CSV from your previous tool and import them under Settings, Packages, Products and Addresses. Order history is not imported; keep the old tool's export for reference. Automation rules must be rebuilt by hand because other tools evaluate rules in a different order.`},
		"migration-cutover":      {"Switching over without missing orders", `Connect your stores to Stowline while the old tool still runs, with Stowline set not to send tracking back. Compare a day's orders in both. On the switch-over day, turn fulfillment off in the old tool, turn tracking back on in Stowline, and void any labels the old tool bought for orders Stowline will ship. Keep the old tool's carrier accounts connected there until every label it bought has been scanned or voided.`},
		"security-data":          {"Where your data is stored", `Data is stored in the United States with a copy in a second US region for disaster recovery; Enterprise accounts can ask for EU residency. Data is encrypted in transit and at rest. Stowline holds a SOC 2 Type II report, available under NDA. Customer addresses are kept as long as the account exists and deleted 30 days after it closes.`},
		"security-partial":       {"Penetration tests and security questionnaires", `We run an external penetration test every year. Summaries are shared with Enterprise customers on request; for security questionnaires, contact your account manager.`},
	}
}
